        <param>
          <type>T</type>
          <declname>defaultValue</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Reads an integer-like environment variable, converting with std::stoi. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>When the variable is absent the default is returned directly. When present, the value is parsed as a signed int and static_cast to T. A parse failure is logged and the default returned. In plain language: &quot;if
the deployment set this variable, try to read it as a whole number; if
that fails for any reason, quietly use the safe default.&quot;</para>
<para><parameterlist kind="templateparam"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>T</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Destination type (typically an unsigned integer or small enum); must be constructible by static_cast from the std::stoi result. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">key</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Environment-variable name. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">defaultValue</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Value used when the variable is absent or unparsable. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>The parsed value, or <computeroutput>defaultValue</computeroutput>. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="note"><para>Because parsing goes through std::stoi, values greater than INT_MAX (or less than INT_MIN) fall back to the default even for 64-bit destinations. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="see"><para><ref refid="classEnvLoader_1a1866cb32c9be29f42152a1c0c9e7b2bb" kindref="member">getEnvironmentVariable(const std::string&amp;, const
std::chrono::seconds&amp;)</ref> </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
